msgid "SIGNALS_1_GUIDE_NAME"
msgstr "Placing Signals"

msgid "SIGNALS_1_GUIDE_PARAGRAPH"
msgstr ""
"If you want to run more than one train on your line, you'll usually need to "
"place signals. A train will only drive, if it can reserve the entire path "
"from its current position to the next signal or station.\n"
"Once you've built your switches and passing siding, place a signal on each "
"of the two tracks. Make sure that the signal on one track points in one "
"direction, while the one on the other track points in the opposite "
"direction. This way, your train knows which track to use when traveling in "
"one direction or the other."

msgid "SIGNALS_2_GUIDE_NAME"
msgstr "How to Use Signals"

msgid "SIGNALS_2_GUIDE_PARAGRAPH"
msgstr ""
"On longer passing sidings, you can place multiple signals along the same "
"track to divide it into separate segments. This lets several trains travel "
"on the same side at once. Each train stops at its own signal instead of "
"waiting for the entire siding to clear. The more signals you add, the more "
"trains can travel on your line."

